---
from hyperflask import request
from hyperflask_users import current_user
from app.models import db

def post():
    with db:
        current_user.tutorial_completed = True
        current_user.save()
        current_user.update_brevo()
    return ""
---
<div id="tutorial" x-data="{step: 0}" class="alert bg-info text-black flex mb-5">
    <div x-show="step==0" class="flex justify-between flex-1 items-center">
        <span><{Icon "question-circle-fill" class="mr-2"}/> SQLIFY uses SQLETL, a variant of SQL with added features to perform queries against any data sources</span>
        <div>
            <{Button as="a" href="https://sqlifyme.github.io/sqlify/" target="_blank" class="btn-ghost btn-sm"}>Read docs</{}>
            <button class="btn btn-sm btn-primary" onclick="document.getElementById('tutorial-video').showModal()"><{Icon "play-fill"}/> Watch intro video</button>
            <button class="btn btn-sm btn-primary" @click="step=1">Start tutorial</button>
            <{Button class="btn-sm btn-ghost" hx_post=url_for('Tutorial') hx_target="#tutorial" hx_swap="delete"}>✕</{}>
        </div>
        <dialog id="tutorial-video" class="modal">
            <div class="modal-box w-9/12 aspect-video p-0 max-w-none">
                <iframe width="100%" height="100%" src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/nfMXSioijxo?si=vqRkXd3rYgjWqapX" title="YouTube video player" frameborder="0"
                allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share"
                referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin" allowfullscreen></iframe>
            </div>
            <form method="dialog" class="modal-backdrop">
                <button>close</button>
            </form>
        </dialog>
    </div>
    <div x-show="step==1" class="prose flex-1 max-w-full">
        <p>
            SQLETL is a superset of SQL that allows you to query many network accessible data sources (HTTP APIs, other databases, CSV files, ...)
        </p>
        <p>
            Let's start with an example:
        </p>
        <{TutorialQuery query="step1.1"}/>
        <p>
            Here we are:
            <ol>
                <li>Querying the URL <a href="https://geo.api.gouv.fr/communes">https://geo.api.gouv.fr/communes</a> using a query parameter "codePostal". This endpoint returns JSON data</li>
                <li>Filtering the data on the "population" property</li>
                <li>Only returning the "nom" and "population" properties</li>
            </ol>
        </p>
        <p>
            When building your WHERE clause, query parameters must be prefixed by a question mark.<br>
            It is important to note that a single SQLETL query may perform multiple queries to retrieve needed data.
            For example when using OR conditions with query parameters:
        </p>
        <{TutorialQuery query="step1.2"}/>
        <p>
            The "EXPLAIN" command is available and will show the ETL pipeline to be executed.
            <{a href="#" hx_post=url_for('TutorialQuery', query="step1.2", explain=1) hx_target="body" hx_swap="beforeend"}>See last example plan</{}>
        </p>
        <div class="flex justify-between align-items mt-6">
            <a href="/docs/select" target="_blank">Read more on how to query different data sources</a>
            <button @click="step=2" class="btn btn-primary">Next <{Icon "arrow-right-circle-fill"}/></button>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div x-show="step==2" class="prose flex-1 max-w-full">
        <p>
            When querying various APIs, you will most likely need to transform their response.
        </p>
        <p>
            Use the URL fragment to extract a portion of the response as the results.
        </p>
        <{TutorialQuery query="step2.1"}/>
        <p>
            Use the <code>EXPAND</code> function to expand a hash into fields.
        </p>
        <{TutorialQuery query="step2.2"}/>
        <p>
            Use the <code>UNNEST</code> function to transform a sub array to rows
        </p>
        <{TutorialQuery query="step2.3"}/>
        <div class="flex justify-between align-items mt-6">
            <a href="/docs/select#processing-the-response" target="_blank">Read more on SQLETL transformation capabilities</a>
            <button @click="step=3" class="btn btn-primary">Next <{Icon "arrow-right-circle-fill"}/></button>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div x-show="step==3" class="prose flex-1 max-w-full">
        <p>
            Finally, you can use the power of SQL to JOIN, use WINDOW functions and more.
        </p>
        <{TutorialQuery query="step3.1"}/>
        <p>
            CTEs are supported. They are usefull when you need to process a result in multiple setps.
        </p>
        <{TutorialQuery query="step3.2"}/>
        <div class="flex justify-between align-items mt-6">
            <a href="/docs" target="_blank">SQLETL full reference</a>
            <{Button hx_post=url_for('Tutorial') hx_target="#tutorial" hx_swap="delete" style="primary"}>Finish</{}>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
